A stack supports `push` and `pop` according to LIFO. What does this code output?

```java
Stack s = new Stack();
s.push(9);
s.push(3);
int right = s.pop();
int left = s.pop();
s.push(left - right);
s.push(2);
right = s.pop();
left = s.pop();
s.push(left * right);
write(s.pop());
```

A stack evaluates postfix expressions; for `-`, the right and left operands are popped in that order. What does the code output?

```java
Stack s = new Stack();
s.push(9);
s.push(3);
s.push(2);
int right = s.pop();
int left = s.pop();
s.push(left * right);
right = s.pop();
left = s.pop();
s.push(left - right);
write(s.pop());
```

With classic `switch` syntax, without `break`, execution continues into the next case. The stack is initially empty. What is in the stack afterward (top first)?

```java
Stack s = new Stack();
int left = 3;
int right = 5;
switch ('+') {
    case '+': s.push(left + right);
    case '*': s.push(left * right);
        break;
}
```

A stack processes the operands and operators in this postfix expression from left to right. What does the code output?

```java
Stack s = new Stack();
s.push(8);
s.push(4);
s.push(2);
int right = s.pop();
int left = s.pop();
s.push(left - right);
right = s.pop();
left = s.pop();
s.push(left * right);
write(s.pop());
```

Java reference assignments copy a reference. Therefore, `a` and `b` point to the same stack. What does the code output?

```java
Stack a = new Stack();
a.push(2);
Stack b = a;
b.push(9);
write(a.pop());
```

A stack is LIFO, and a queue is FIFO. What does the code output?

```java
Stack s = new Stack();
Queue q = new Queue();
s.push(4);
s.push(7);
q.enqueue(4);
q.enqueue(7);
int x = s.pop();
int y = q.dequeue();
write(x - y);
```
